## 416eab72 Keep the node's messages in its volume until the server confirms them

A node used to keep at most 50 unconfirmed job ends in memory and lost every other message when it restarted or the
server did. Now every message in NUMBERED goes through an outbox file (outbox.jsonl in the data folder, mode 0600, root
owned by secureNode). Each gets the next number of a stream made with the file and stays until ack { upto }, or, for a
job's or a part's end, until job.ack or tests.ack too. A job's end is held from the moment the job leaves running and is
numbered when its turn comes. Past 20,000 entries the oldest job.event goes first and an end is never dropped. A last
line cut short is cut off on open, and the next number stays above everything the file held.

The hello carries the stream, lists the jobs and parts whose ends wait, and every pending entry is sent in order after
it. With the Hornhoof-Acks header on the upgrade answer, ack drops what it confirms; without it (a server of the build
before) what was sent is dropped at once but for the ends, as before. The terminal host already sends through emit, so
its numbered messages (job.program, agent.unheard, agent.hook_rejected, agent.context) are covered with no change to it.

The connection waits at most 10 s between tries, 60 s after a refusal, and gives up a handshake after 20 s. Its onOpen
tells whether the server confirms.

Ruling: host.ts is not changed - why: its sends already go through emit, which numbers by type - cost if wrong: a numbered message of the host sent outside emit would go unnumbered.
Ruling: the first end held for an id stays, a second is ignored - why: Hornhoof finishes a job once, and the old map replaced the end only to the same effect - cost if wrong: a later, different end of the same job is never sent.
Ruling: the file is a stream line, hold lines and add lines, and drops are written by the rewrite after flushMs, not by a line each - why: the plan's own words - cost if wrong: a crash within a second of an ack sends a confirmed message again, which the server lists and skips.
Ruling: one old assertion in main.test.ts (toContainEqual of a job.event) became objectContaining - why: the message now carries n - cost if wrong: none, it still checks every other field.
Ruling: the test of the older server waits for the node to see its socket gone before the program talks - why: a message sent into a dead socket is given up by design with a server that confirms nothing - cost if wrong: none.

